# Normalise line endings at checkout, not in the repository, so that the same
# blob works on every platform someone builds PicoLM on.
#
# Why this file exists: tools/msvc_build.bat was committed with LF-only line
# endings. cmd.exe tolerates LF for simple commands, but its label lookup
# (goto :label / call :label) scans the file as a byte stream and mis-handles
# LF-only files, so the build died with
#
#   The system cannot find the batch label specified - parse_args
#
# before compiling anything. Every .bat here relies on goto/call labels, so
# they must be CRLF on disk regardless of what the repository stores.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.btm           text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must stay LF, or the kernel refuses them (bad interpreter) and
# a CRLF-checked-out ./script.sh fails in a way that looks like a missing
# interpreter rather than a line-ending problem.
*.sh            text eol=lf
configure       text eol=lf

# Sources: LF on disk EVERYWHERE, explicitly.
#
# This used to be a bare "text", i.e. normalise on commit and let the platform
# default apply on checkout. On Windows that means CRLF in the working tree
# while the blob is LF, so git reports every line of every .c/.h file as
# modified the moment it is checked out. Here it showed up as
# picolm/steer.c permanently "M" with a 179/179 diff, which blocked
# `git stash pop` and, worse, stopped an in-progress `git rebase` dead on a
# file nobody had edited. Committing the file once did not help -- the next
# checkout re-expanded it.
#
# Same failure as the .bat rule above, inverted: there the working tree needs
# CRLF, here it must not. eol=lf makes the checkout deterministic.
*.c             text eol=lf
*.h             text eol=lf
*.hpp           text eol=lf
*.cuh           text eol=lf
*.cu            text eol=lf
*.mm            text eol=lf
*.m             text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf

# Binary-ish assets: never touch these.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.gguf          binary
*.o             binary
*.exe           binary
